notes.body:
That merge request is now closed; how much, if any, of it applies given:

- #16221 and !11997 for Windows (which depends on Qt 6.5 or later, I think; we're building the main branch on Windows with 6.5.2);
- #15511 and whatever changes were made for it for macOS (also, I think, depends on Qt 6.5, with which we're building the main branch on macOS);
- #18246 for X11 and Wayland, modulo the issues with different window managers/compositors and toolkits and....

If none, presumably this thread should be resolved.

Or just extend the existing "coloringrules" format to support *four* colors, the first two being the light-mode foreground and background colors and the next two being the dark-mode foreground and background colors, and:

- have the code that reads it try parsing the colors portion of the line as either [Rfg,Gfg,Bfg][Rbg,Gbg,Bbg] or [Rfgl,Gfgl,Bfgl][Rbgl,Gbgl,Bbgl][Rfgd,Gfgd,Bfgd][Rbgd,Gbgd,Bbgd] and, for the first of those formats, algorithmically computing the dark-mode colors;
- have the code that writes it write it out in the second format;

as it appears that, if I add two additional colors to the end of the line, the existing code will read it and ignore the additional colors.

> Switching fg/bg is an old-style color mode and not really applicable to modern UIs.

How do modern UIs allow you to, for example, colorize entries in lists in a particular fashion?  Do they, for example, let you select a color to be used as the background color, and then use an appropriate foreground color for text against that background color?

("Appropriate" meaning "with enough contrast that Old People like me can read the text without too much difficulty, so that we don't feel like marching into the office of the people who chose that color pattern and forcing them to, when making UI decisions, wear goggles that impair their ability to read in low-contrast situations" - yes, that *really is* a thing, e.g. with the nitwits responsible for Apple's Notes app defaulting to light yellow as its "highlight color", so that web links in the text of a note are light yellow on white, which is close to unreadable for Old People like me.)

> Also, this will lead to scenarios, where Wireshark will break consistency with the rest of the system, as it will look vastly different compared to other apps.

Are there no other apps that allow the user to select colors?  Apple's Mail app, for example, allows the user to select:

- the color to be used for, at least, the first three levels of quoting in displayed messages;
- the color to be used to "highlight messages when not grouped" (I'm not sure what that means, as I've configured Mail to group messages into threads);
- the color to be applied to a message when a particular user-defined filtering rule matches a message;

although it does not, unfortunately, allow me to add additional colors for "flags" to apply to messages.

So in which of the places in which we allow colors to be selected does that feature allow Wireshark to "break consistency with the rest of the system"?  (I'm not seeing any way where we don't *currently* allow that but Stig's suggestion would then result in us allowing it.)

> Additionally, I also do not think that we should further go down the road of color filters for UI. The color filters should apply to the dissection only

Where has anybody suggested that the coloring rules apply to anything other than the dissection?

> The underlying problem is neither solved by this change nor by storing the preference in the profile though. The underlying problem is, that we do not apply and use the Qt style system correctly, but rather implement our own. To do this right, we should abandon this approach and move to the Qt style system (except for the coloring of our results).

In what places are we not using the Qt style system?  The places I know of where we allow users to select colors are:

- marked packet foreground and background colors;
- ignored packet foreground and background colors;
- "Follow Stream" client text;
- "Follow Stream" server text;
- valid filter background color;
- invalid filter background color;
- valid-but-with-a-warning background color;
- coloring rules;
- I/O graphs.

For which of those does the Qt style system specify colors?  The only ones where the use isn't specific to Wireshark (or to packet analysis tools) are the background colors for the filter.

And what does "the coloring of our results" mean here?  Does it including coloring rules?  (I'm guessing it does, as I doubt the Qt style system has much to say about the appropriate color for "packets that match the expression 'ip.ttl > 10'".)

ADDED:

Or do you just mean "allow the user either to select a background color, and let the Qt style system select the foreground color to be used and select what background color to use if the light/dark mode changes from the one you were in when selecting the color to the opposite mode, or select a foreground color (leaving it up to the user to select one that works with the given background color), and let the Qt style system select what foreground color to use if the light/dark mode changes from the one you were in when selecting the color to the opposite mode"?

If so, I think *that* would be OK.  (Refusing to allow the user to do *any* color selection - or, for cases such as coloring rules and I/O graphs, allowing them only to select fro a small seet of colors rather than letting them select *arbitrary* colors if they choose - would not be OK.  Apple Mail, for example, allows that, including, for example, "here's a color wheel, have fun!"  It doesn't allow selecting both foreground *and* background color for a mail rule, but it allows choosing one of them to set.)

